WEBVTT

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:02.879
One point two five billion dollars.

00:00:02.829 --> 00:00:07.909
That is the total in political contributions tracked from Elections Canada's own open data.

00:00:07.909 --> 00:00:09.465
Every dollar.

00:00:09.465 --> 00:00:11.011
Every donor.

00:00:11.011 --> 00:00:12.568
Every party.

00:00:12.568 --> 00:00:15.136
From 2004 to the present.

00:00:15.136 --> 00:00:20.602
One hundred and thirty-four registered third parties spending money to influence Canadian elections.

00:00:20.602 --> 00:00:25.329
Six thousand active lobbyists working across all parties simultaneously.

00:00:25.329 --> 00:00:30.079
The money flows in from every direction — and this dashboard shows you where it goes.

00:00:30.079 --> 00:00:32.340
Party-by-party fundraising.

00:00:32.340 --> 00:00:35.568
Year-over-year trends going back two decades.

00:00:35.568 --> 00:00:40.556
The data is unambiguous: political money in Canada does not flow randomly.

00:00:40.556 --> 00:00:42.556
It follows patterns.

00:00:42.556 --> 00:00:47.795
It concentrates in specific parties, specific ridings, and specific election cycles.

00:00:47.795 --> 00:00:54.136
Every chart, every table, every number on this page comes directly from Elections Canada's published records.

00:00:54.136 --> 00:00:56.511
The government collected this data.

00:00:56.511 --> 00:01:00.443
We organized it so you could see the pattern they hoped you wouldn't notice.
